If you're getting "Claims: 0" (empty claims) in your ASP.NET Core API when verifying an Azure B2C token, follow these steps to debug and fix it:


---

1. Decode and Verify Your Token

Copy your JWT token and paste it into jwt.io.

Check if the payload section contains claims like:

{
  "oid": "1234-abcd-5678-efgh",
  "sub": "abcd-1234-efgh-5678",
  "name": "Pankaj Bhasin",
  "email": "pankaj@example.com"
}

If claims are missing:

Ensure you're requesting the correct scopes when getting the token from Azure B2C.




---

2. Update TokenValidationParameters

If your token has claims but ASP.NET Core doesn't recognize them, modify Program.cs to explicitly include claim mappings:

builder.Services.AddAuthentication(JwtBearerDefaults.AuthenticationScheme)
    .AddJwtBearer(options =>
    {
        options.Authority = "https://yourtenant.b2clogin.com/yourtenant.onmicrosoft.com/v2.0/";
        options.Audience = "your-client-id";
        options.TokenValidationParameters = new TokenValidationParameters
        {
            ValidateIssuer = true,
            ValidIssuer = "https://yourtenant.b2clogin.com/{tenant-id}/v2.0/",
            ValidAudience = "your-client-id",
            RoleClaimType = "roles",  // Map role claims correctly
            NameClaimType = "name"     // Ensure name claim is available
        };
    });

ValidAudience must match your Azure AD B2C App's Client ID.

NameClaimType and RoleClaimType ensure claims are correctly mapped.



---

3. Check API Permissions in Azure B2C

1. Go to Azure Portal → Azure AD B2C → App Registrations.


2. Select your API App Registration.


3. Go to "API Permissions" → Ensure it has "openid", "profile", and "email" scopes.


4. If missing, click "Add Permissions" and assign these.




---

4. Log Claims in the Controller

Modify your API to log all claims and see what's being received:

[HttpGet("test")]
public IActionResult Test()
{
    var user = HttpContext.User;
    if (!user.Identity.IsAuthenticated)
    {
        return Unauthorized("User is not authenticated");
    }

    var claims = user.Claims.Select(c => new { c.Type, c.Value }).ToList();

    if (claims.Count == 0)
    {
        return BadRequest("No claims found in token.");
    }

    return Ok(claims);
}

Call /test with your B2C token in Postman (Authorization: Bearer YOUR_TOKEN).

If it returns "No claims found in token", check Step 1 & 2.



---

5. Ensure Token Contains Claims via B2C Policy

If your token doesn't have claims, update your Azure B2C User Flow:

1. Go to Azure AD B2C → User Flows.


2. Select your SignUpSignIn Policy (or any relevant policy).


3. Go to Application Claims and enable:

email

givenName

surname

objectId

roles (if using roles).



4. Save and run the user flow again to get a new token.




---

Final Steps

1. Decode token at jwt.io → Ensure claims exist.


2. Modify TokenValidationParameters in Program.cs.


3. Enable claims in Azure B2C policy.


4. Log claims in API to debug missing values.



